-- title:
--   A regular local ring is a domain
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring which is a regular local ring, that is, a Noetherian local ring whose maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$ satisfies $\dim_{R/\mathfrak m}\mathfrak m/\mathfrak m^2 = \dim R$ (equivalently, $\mathfrak m$ is generated by $\dim R$ elements). The conclusion is that $R$ is an integral domain in the sense of Mathlib's `IsDomain`: $R$ is nontrivial ($0 \neq 1$) and has no zero divisors, so that $xy = 0$ forces $x = 0$ or $y = 0$ and multiplication by a nonzero element is cancellative. The regularity hypothesis is carried as a typeclass assumption on $R$, and the conclusion is produced as an instance-style statement, so that it is available wherever a ring has been shown to be regular local.
--
--   This is the classical fact that regular local rings are domains (Matsumura, Theorem 14.3), proved by induction on the embedding dimension after cutting by an element of $\mathfrak m \setminus \mathfrak m^2$ avoiding the minimal primes. It is used throughout the commutative-algebra input to the patching argument, for instance when identifying localisations of adic completions at non-maximal primes as fields or as regular local rings, and when producing regular pairs in two-dimensional integrally closed rings.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IsRegularLocalRing_isDomain.lean

